$DIR/main.p:8:17: cannot pass ptrs(1) to the function value (which may call the loop body at main.p:8), which keeps it after returning
    ptrs(1) points into the closure ptrs(1) returns, built in main's frame.
    ptrs(1) returns a closure, which it builds, with the variables it uses, in a
    region of its caller's frame, main's (at main.p:8); they live as long as
    that frame (section 11.5). the function value (which may call the loop body
    at main.p:8) stores argument 1 in package variable saved (main.p:9), so
    ptrs(1) would outlive main's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change the function value (which may call the loop body at main.p:8) to take
    a value or an owner, which it can keep: an owned[T] from new, a shared[T],
    or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
